# rule.always.reuse-pavement-before-improvise

before you improvise an approach, look for the paved one — and when it exists, reuse it.

pavement = any artifact a past traveler left so the next need not re-derive: a brief, a howto, a template, a skill, a route, a term cluster, a resolved dispute, a rule.

## .where to look — cheapest first, stop at the first hit

1. this repo's briefs — `rhx grepsafe --pattern '<concept>' --path src --glob '*.md'`
2. `domain.terms/` — is the word settled? is a dispute resolved?
3. skills, templates, routes — `rhx globsafe --pattern '.agent/**/skills/*'`
4. the org — `rhx git.repo.get lines --repos 'ehmpathy/*' --words '<concept>'`

- `--glob` matches the basename. a `/` in it matches no file, and reports that as a clean `0 matches`, exit 0 — indistinguishable from a genuine absence. scope with `--path`
- a dot-prefixed file may be skipped, so a `.readme.md` can be invisible to it
- the pattern, which survives both repairs above. prose is the worst corpus for a literal match: markdown puts `**` and backticks between the letters (`FAIL` misses `**FAIL**`), a writer inflects freely (`withdrawn` misses `WITHDREW`), and a settled rename leaves the old word live only in memory (`cast*` misses `as*`)
- and one miss is structural, never unlucky: the layer. a blackbox test asserts on rendered output, so it carries the human's word and cannot carry the identifier. an identifier search finds every white-box test and misses every black-box one, which is the coverage you went to look for

read an empty result as evidence about the pattern first, the corpus second. a false positive is dismissed in seconds; a false negative returns silence, so it is believed — which makes real the braided trail the check was meant to prevent.

where the answer must be complete, settle it with a `Glob` or a `Read`. `globsafe` shares neither tool defect; its `--pattern` takes a full path, so the `globsafe` line above is right as written.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you would write a step-by-step for a task this repo has done | grep, do not type |
| you would say "the way to do this is…" | that sentence is a claim the repo may already own |
| you name a new artifact, dir, or convention | check what the repo already names |
| you reach for a workaround because a paved path is inconvenient | that is the braid. mend the tread instead |
| you would cite a brief as a parent claim | glob for it first — a `.see also` is a promise |
| a peer repo has a convention | look, then test it. precedent is a reason to look, never to conform |
| a search comes back empty and that zero would change your verdict | read the file. the pattern is the likelier defect, and its failure is silent |
| a search matches two words in one file and you read a relation into it | it reports co-occurrence. a search proposes; only a read disposes |

test: "has this repo solved this already, and where would it have put the answer?" — you can name the likely path → look there. you genuinely cannot → improvise, and leave a route behind.

## .when improvisation is right

no pavement exists (owes a route) · the pavement is measurably wrong (then fix it, do not walk beside it) · it is for a different bounded context (say so, and why).

## .do not let this stall

"look first", not "read it all." bounded = a grep, a glob of the obvious dir, one likely brief. two empty searches discharge the rule.

two empty searches with the same pattern are one search. the bound above is a budget, never a licence to believe a silence: vary the pattern — a second inflection, the emphasis stripped, the word this repo renamed from — before the second zero counts. otherwise you discharge the rule on a pattern defect and improvise beside pavement that was there all along.

blocker: a conclusion re-derived that the repo had settled, with no look · a second artifact laid beside a serviceable one · a brief cited with no file behind it (the phantom path) · a paved path judged wrong and worked around rather than fixed · a verdict changed on a search's silence where one read would have settled it. nitpick: an unbounded survey that stalls the work · two empty searches that differ in no way but the file they scanned.

## .the anti-patterns

- the braided trail — a second path beside a serviceable one
- the rut — a found convention adopted with no test that it works (the mirror failure: you did look, and conformed anyway)
- the phantom path — a cited parent claim that was never written

## a phantom path is not merely an unresolvable name — mark the foreign one

| the citation | what it is | the repair |
|---|---|---|
| a parent claim with no file | a phantom. the argument rests on a premise nobody wrote | write it, or drop the claim |
| a prescribed artifact — a path the rule tells you to create | not a citation. it names a future file | state the full path |
| a foreign artifact — in a peer repo | legitimate, and only if marked | say it is foreign, in the same breath |

test, of every backticked name: "does my argument rest on this, or merely report it?" rests → it must exist here · reports → say where it lives, in the same sentence.

`philosophy.pavement-saves-nature` is the why. `rule.always.enskill-the-tactics-you-discover` is what you owe when no pavement was found.
